WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.500
Movies with a low rating on Rotten Tomatoes
are common.

00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:07.210
But to get a 0 percent rating takes a certain
kind of awfulness.

00:00:07.210 --> 00:00:12.230
Sure, it's usually not the type of perfect
the filmmakers were going for, but to get

00:00:12.230 --> 00:00:16.530
any kind of perfectly unanimous response on
a film is quite a feat.

00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:20.810
Though watching a bad movie on purpose can
seem like a complete waste of time, some of

00:00:20.810 --> 00:00:23.480
these just have to be seen to be believed.

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:28.240
Find out how things can go so wrong, so fast
with these critical bombs you should watch

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:29.240
anyway.

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:31.730
Manos: The Hands of Fate

00:00:31.730 --> 00:00:37.050
It's one of the best episodes of Mystery Science
Theater 3000 and one of the strangest films

00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:38.149
of all time.

00:00:38.149 --> 00:00:42.390
From the beginning, everything about Manos:
The Hands of Fate was a bad idea.

00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:46.870
Hal Warren told Oscar-winning screenwriter
Stirling Silliphant that making a movie was

00:00:46.870 --> 00:00:47.870
easy.

00:00:47.870 --> 00:00:52.750
He figured it was so simple, he bet Silliphant
that even he, a lowly fertilizer salesman,

00:00:52.750 --> 00:00:54.059
could finish a picture.

00:00:54.059 --> 00:00:58.770
When your grand artistic vision stems from
winning a bet, you're already off to a bad

00:00:58.770 --> 00:00:59.770
start.

00:00:59.770 --> 00:01:04.860
So Warren raised $19,000 from friends and
family and started filming Manos.

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:10.060
He crafted a complicated story about a family
that gets lost on vacation and stumbles upon

00:01:10.060 --> 00:01:15.249
a cult leader that tries to turn all the women
in his family to his stable of bickering brides.

00:01:15.249 --> 00:01:17.820
“You are a trouble maker.

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:21.459
You deserve to die!”

00:01:21.459 --> 00:01:24.130
It could’ve been something simple, but it
wasn’t.

00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:26.409
Manos is definitely not a cash grab.

00:01:26.409 --> 00:01:29.369
Instead, it’s a bizarre expression of art.

00:01:29.369 --> 00:01:33.950
Though everything about it is strange, there
did seem to be some attempt at making a real

00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:34.950
film.

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:39.029
At the end of the day, Manos is a movie about
a real family coming together, giving their

00:01:39.029 --> 00:01:44.159
all, and trying to make art no matter how
low-budget or strange.

00:01:44.159 --> 00:01:45.349
Roller Boogie

00:01:45.349 --> 00:01:50.020
This disco-infused roller skating wreck was
supposed to be a breakout role for Exorcist

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:51.490
alum Linda Blair.

00:01:51.490 --> 00:01:55.700
If she wanted to get as far away from her
Exorcist casting as possible, the idea of

00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:59.539
doing a harmless roller skating picture doesn't
sound like a bad idea.

00:01:59.539 --> 00:02:00.539
Right?

00:02:00.539 --> 00:02:03.779
“Now I can’t wait.”

00:02:03.779 --> 00:02:06.439
Okay, maybe it was a bad idea.

00:02:06.439 --> 00:02:10.299
Unfortunately, everyone hated the film, and
it did little to lift her career.

00:02:10.299 --> 00:02:11.299
Roger Ebert wrote,

00:02:11.299 --> 00:02:16.129
"I didn't think it was still possible, in
the dog-eared final days of the 1970s, to

00:02:16.129 --> 00:02:19.670
have this silly, innocent, lame-brained and
naive movie."

00:02:19.670 --> 00:02:24.220
The fact that the film contained all disco
music, which was fading in popularity, didn't

00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:25.290
help matters.

00:02:25.290 --> 00:02:30.909
Xanadu came out the next year, just to double-check
that the nation was truly over roller dancing.

00:02:30.909 --> 00:02:33.680
Yeah … we were over it.

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:34.680
Heartbeeps

00:02:36.460 --> 00:02:38.640
"I'm here to tell you about this new movie.

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:42.360
It’s called Heartbeeps, and it’s about
me and my pals."

00:02:42.370 --> 00:02:47.980
Andy Kaufman and Bernadette Peters star as
two robots who were created to be human companions.

00:02:47.980 --> 00:02:50.959
When they meet each other, they fall in love
and run away.

00:02:50.959 --> 00:02:52.370
Which is kind of cute.

00:02:52.370 --> 00:02:54.890
But that's not the whole story of Heartbeeps.

00:02:54.890 --> 00:03:00.109
There's also a tank-like robot called Crimebuster
who's come to hunt them down, a garbage child

00:03:00.109 --> 00:03:05.829
robot that Kaufman made from spare parts,
and Catskil, a corny stand-up comedian robot

00:03:05.829 --> 00:03:07.519
who serves no purpose whatsoever.

00:03:07.519 --> 00:03:11.120
"But believe me folks, this movie’s got
a lot goin' for it."

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:14.950
Though the movie is weird and a lot of the
jokes are hokey, what makes it all the more

00:03:14.950 --> 00:03:17.049
strange is the robot design.

00:03:17.049 --> 00:03:21.480
Peters looks like some kind of overly-oily
first-generation fembot.

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:25.639
Kaufman's robot could be a carbon copy of
a too-tan Chevy Chase.

00:03:25.639 --> 00:03:29.730
Kaufman appeared in so few films, it's worth
watching Heartbeeps for that alone, and it

00:03:29.730 --> 00:03:34.579
also serves as a warning that even the funniest
people can manage to make awfully unfunny

00:03:34.579 --> 00:03:36.150
things.

00:03:36.150 --> 00:03:38.040
Megaforce

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:42.330
Star Wars is one of the most influential films
of all time, but one of the biggest effects

00:03:42.330 --> 00:03:47.120
of the sci-fi blockbuster was the crazy amount
of ripoffs that followed in its wake, like

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:49.409
the epic failure Megaforce.

00:03:49.409 --> 00:03:52.829
Megaforce is about, you know, explosions and
laser guns.

00:03:52.829 --> 00:03:56.859
It's hard to admit, but even after multiple
viewings, the only thing from Megaforce that

00:03:56.859 --> 00:04:00.560
will be mega-impressed upon your brain is
Barry Bostwick's booty.

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:03.639
Now, that's not to say this film isn't worth
watching.

00:04:03.639 --> 00:04:07.980
The weird mix of comedy and action plus the
utter lack of a budget make this a pretty

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:08.980
funny watch.

00:04:08.980 --> 00:04:13.040
You won't get some intricate tale of space
intrigue, but you will get to see one of the

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:15.670
worst flying motorcycle special effects.

00:04:15.670 --> 00:04:22.000
“I can’t understand / But I’ve only
been so far.”

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:23.949
Seriously, who thought that looked good?

00:04:23.949 --> 00:04:28.569
Plus, there's the added bonus Barry Bostwick
with bleached hair looking like a rejected

00:04:28.569 --> 00:04:29.569
Gibb brother.

00:04:29.569 --> 00:04:33.479
As the New York Times' review of the film
put it, "The combination is original — just

00:04:33.479 --> 00:04:38.770
as a peanut butter and olive loaf sandwich
might be — but it isn't any good."

00:04:38.770 --> 00:04:40.400
Staying Alive

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:44.979
Though you may only remember Saturday Night
Fever for its disco dancing and white bell

00:04:44.979 --> 00:04:48.979
bottoms, it's actually a really great film
about lower-class kids trying to find some

00:04:48.979 --> 00:04:50.990
good and hope in their lives.

00:04:50.990 --> 00:04:55.330
As the sequel to Saturday Night Fever, Staying
Alive has none of that.

00:04:55.330 --> 00:05:00.460
In Staying Alive, Tony Manero has moved to
Manhattan and wants to be a dancer on Broadway.

00:05:00.460 --> 00:05:05.389
Unlike the first film where Tony is a charming
but troubled young man, here Tony is just

00:05:05.389 --> 00:05:06.900
an all-the-time jerk.

00:05:06.900 --> 00:05:11.330
Now in addition to the Bee Gees, we get the
dulcet tones of Frank Stallone, brother of

00:05:11.330 --> 00:05:13.889
Sylvester Stallone — who directed the film.

00:05:13.889 --> 00:05:19.610
The dance numbers are bizarre, the leotards
are cut high, and the story is just garbage.

00:05:19.610 --> 00:05:21.650
But it's great garbage!

00:05:21.650 --> 00:05:23.330
MAC and Me

00:05:23.330 --> 00:05:28.259
Another film from the ripoff department, this
D-grade E.T. has the double distinction of

00:05:28.259 --> 00:05:33.259
trying to capitalize on Spielberg's incredibly
successful film and being a great excuse for

00:05:33.259 --> 00:05:34.910
blatant product placement.

00:05:34.910 --> 00:05:39.729
MAC stands for Mysterious Alien Creature,
and it turns out that Coca-Cola, not Reese's

00:05:39.729 --> 00:05:45.139
Pieces, pleases this alien, although MAC is
way more fond of product placement than his

00:05:45.139 --> 00:05:46.599
Spielbergian counterpart.

00:05:46.599 --> 00:05:51.910
A whole scene is devoted to a birthday party
held at McDonald's that features Ronald McDonald

00:05:51.910 --> 00:05:57.300
himself, and enough of the chain's logos to
fund the production company's next five knockoffs.

00:05:57.300 --> 00:06:02.690
There's also a dance number, which ET is notably
lacking, so at least one thing was different.

00:06:02.690 --> 00:06:10.490
“A friend in the end / Is always there / To
give their heart”

00:06:10.490 --> 00:06:13.449
Seriously, it just goes on like that.

00:06:13.449 --> 00:06:18.360
Another reason to see this one: Almost every
time Paul Rudd goes on Conan to promote a

00:06:18.360 --> 00:06:23.160
film, he switches out the clip of his own
movie with the same clip of MAC and Me.

00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:28.050
Rudd has done this for over a decade, and
for some reason it never gets old.

00:06:28.050 --> 00:06:29.530
Simon Sez

00:06:29.530 --> 00:06:34.300
Simply watching a Simon toy light up for an
hour and a half would contain better acting

00:06:34.300 --> 00:06:36.360
than this Dennis Rodman sequel.

00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:40.990
The original, entitled Double Team, featured
the bad boy basketball player and Jean-Claude

00:06:40.990 --> 00:06:44.870
Van Damme teaming up to kick butt and give
horrible line readings.

00:06:44.870 --> 00:06:46.979
"Their styles are different.

00:06:46.979 --> 00:06:48.240
Very different."

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:53.340
For the sequel, Van Damme did not return,
so they got the next best thing: Dane Cook.

00:06:53.340 --> 00:06:59.830
The movie has a lot of "comedy" and plenty
of Rodman looking cool while super fake explosions

00:06:59.830 --> 00:07:01.080
happen behind him.

00:07:01.080 --> 00:07:05.520
The film is a great reminder of a time when
Rodman was a really big deal.

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:09.160
1999 feels so, so far away.

00:07:09.160 --> 00:07:10.770
Pinocchio

00:07:10.770 --> 00:07:15.050
If you want to see the second-worst film of
all time, sit down for a terrible evening

00:07:15.050 --> 00:07:16.050
with Pinocchio.

00:07:16.050 --> 00:07:18.110
No, not the Disney version.

00:07:18.110 --> 00:07:20.900
The 2002 Roberto Benigni vehicle.

00:07:20.900 --> 00:07:25.919
After winning two Oscars for Life is Beautiful,
America was charmed by this excitable Italian

00:07:25.919 --> 00:07:29.420
and couldn't wait for what wonderful film
he'd bring to us next.

00:07:29.420 --> 00:07:33.080
Unfortunately, the next film he brought was
this one.

00:07:33.080 --> 00:07:35.060
“It’s Pinocchio!”

00:07:35.060 --> 00:07:36.060
“Pinocchio!”

00:07:36.060 --> 00:07:40.880
It's odd enough that a 49-year-old Benigni
decided to play the small puppet child, but

00:07:40.880 --> 00:07:43.539
the American version had to make it even weirder.

00:07:43.539 --> 00:07:48.099
Instead of dubbing the film with Benigni's
accented voice in English, Breckin Meyer of

00:07:48.099 --> 00:07:49.909
Road Trip fame was chosen.

00:07:49.909 --> 00:07:54.650
The 30-something voice of Meyer coming out
of the nearly 50-year-old body of Benigni

00:07:54.650 --> 00:07:55.750
is simply disturbing.

00:07:55.750 --> 00:08:01.319
Plus, the fairy tale film has a good moral:
say no to a film where you play a 10-year-old

00:08:01.319 --> 00:08:03.069
if you're pushing 50.

00:08:03.069 --> 00:08:04.919
Ballistic: Ecks vs Sever

00:08:04.919 --> 00:08:10.220
Now, it's true that all these films have a
0 percent rating on Rotten Tomatoes, but the

00:08:10.220 --> 00:08:14.650
crown of worst of the worst has to go to Ballistic:
Ecks vs. Sever.

00:08:14.650 --> 00:08:18.620
A lot of films that reach this level only
have a couple dozen reviews.

00:08:18.620 --> 00:08:23.150
Since there are fewer critics chiming in,
the odds of a perfectly bad score are a little

00:08:23.150 --> 00:08:24.150
better.

00:08:24.150 --> 00:08:28.240
But Ecks vs. Sever was a major release, and
a lot of critics agreed that it deserved the

00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:31.780
ultimate F. 116 critics, to be exact.

00:08:31.780 --> 00:08:34.210
They all agreed this movie was garbage.

00:08:34.210 --> 00:08:37.690
It beats the next worst movie by almost 50
critics.

00:08:37.690 --> 00:08:38.690
Ouch.

00:08:38.690 --> 00:08:40.280
What makes this film so hated?

00:08:40.280 --> 00:08:44.610
Critics seem to agree that the movie has a
convoluted plot, no character development,

00:08:44.610 --> 00:08:49.020
and a ton of messy-looking explosions that
all just blend together after a while.

00:08:49.020 --> 00:08:53.290
Still, it's worth watching just to see what
Rotten Tomatoes considers the very worst of

00:08:53.290 --> 00:08:54.290
them all.

00:08:54.290 --> 00:08:55.530
Thanks for watching!

00:08:55.530 --> 00:08:58.720
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:08:58.720 --> 00:09:01.150
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love too!

